# CORS allowed origins (comma-separated).
#
# READ THIS BEFORE DEPLOYING — where this list comes from depends on how the
# service was deployed, and the two paths behave differently:
#
#   Render (what is live today, server-traininginstitutemanagement.onrender.com):
#     builds from the repo without the Dockerfile, so dotenv reads THIS FILE.
#     Editing the line below and pushing is what actually changes production.
#
#   Docker / Dokploy (docker-compose.production.yml):
#     the runtime stage copies only dist/ and src/templates, so this file is
#     never in the image. CORS_ORIGIN must be set on the platform instead. If
#     it is missing there the API falls back to localhost and every browser
#     request from the real frontend is refused — a boot that looks healthy
#     right up until someone opens the admin panel.
#
# Either way the boot log prints the list actually in force. Check it first.
#
# Rules: scheme + host + port, no trailing slash, no path. http and https are
# different origins. `*` may stand in for one hostname label.
#
# Local dev ports:
#   3011 = admin (Vite)   4001 = frontend (Vite ref)
#   4002 = frontend (Next.js)  3000 = fallback
CORS_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3011,http://localhost:4001,http://localhost:4002,http://localhost:3000,https://admin.zoompropertyltd.com,https://zoompropertyltd.com,https://www.zoompropertyltd.com
# Vercel gives every preview build its own hostname, so a fixed list can never
# contain the URL a tester is looking at. Uncomment to let previews through —
# but note it trusts every project on vercel.app with credentialed requests,
# which is fine for staging and too broad for production.
# CORS_ORIGIN=...,https://*.vercel.app
REVALIDATE_SECRET=training_institute_revalidate_secret
